[10]. On Language Modeling & Fitness Scoring:

      Markov, A. A. (1913).
      An Example of Statistical Investigation of the Text Eugene Onegin
      Concerning the Connection of Samples in Chains.

      Shannon, C. E. (1948).
      A Mathematical Theory of Communication.
      The holy grail of Information Theory, explaining N-grams, entropy,
      and predictability of language.
     
      The GA navigates the immense keyspace of the Steckerbrett,
      while a Markov Chain evaluates the generated text's likelihood of
      being natural language by calculating its N-gram transition probabilities.
     
[11]. On Statistical Cryptanalysis (Fitness Metrics):

      Friedman, W. F. (1922).
      The Index of Coincidence and its Applications in Cryptography.

[12]. On Hidden Markov Models (Advanced Sequence Decoding):

      Baum, L. E., & Petrie, T. (1966).
      Statistical Inference for Probabilistic Functions of Finite State
      Markov Chains.
      
      Rabiner, L. R. (1989).
      A Tutorial on Hidden Markov Models and Selected Applications in
      Speech Recognition. 
      
      While standard Markov Chains model the transition probabilities of
      natural language, this repository implements a Hidden Markov Model (HMM)
      to represent the encryption process itself. The actual plaintext
      characters are treated as the 'Hidden States', while the Enigma''s
      ciphertext output represents the 'Observations'.
      By leveraging the Viterbi Algorithm, the model decodes the most
      likely sequence of hidden states, effectively mathematically stripping
      away the cipher.
    
